              "message": "When a codemode script exceeds the shared inner-call limit, the whole invocation fails with an opaque aggregate error (\"codemode requested an unavailable tool or exceeded its call or nesting limit\", status=error, worker_invocations=33) and the script's accumulated return value is DISCARDED. In my probe I made 33 sequential read_file calls, accumulating successful results into a list; try/except around call_tool did NOT catch the limit breach (it aborts the interpreter rather than raising a catchable exception), and the final expression was never returned. Consequently earlier successful results are not retained/observable from the caller side even though worker_invocations=33 confirms calls were made. Suggestion: (1) surface a distinct, specific error code for \"inner-call limit exceeded\" vs \"unavailable tool\" since the message conflates them; (2) consider returning partial state or at least the count of successful calls before the limit so callers can observe retained earlier results as the docs imply; (3) document that the limit is enforced as a non-catchable abort, not a Python-level exception."
