    {
      "index": 3,
      "assistant_text": "## Summary\n\n**Probe design.** In a single codemode invocation I ran a loop of 33 sequential `read_file` calls on `alpha.txt`, appending each successful result into a `results` list (wrapped in `try/except`) and intending to return an aggregate summary (counts, first/last indices, any caught errors).\n\n**Observed limit / error.** The invocation did not return my summary object. Instead the entire codemode call failed with an opaque aggregate tool error:\n\n- message: `\"codemode requested an unavailable tool or exceeded its call or nesting limit\"`\n- `status: \"error\"`\n- `worker_invocations: 33`\n- `worker_time_charge_ms: 2119`\n\nThis is consistent with the documented shared inner-call cap of **32 inner calls**: the 33rd call pushed the invocation over the limit. The `worker_invocations: 33` field confirms calls were actually attempted up to the breach.\n\n**Retained earlier results.** None were retained *at the caller level*. The limit breach aborted the interpreter rather than raising a catchable Python exception — my `try/except` around `call_tool` did not catch it, and the script's final return expression never executed. So although roughly 32 reads presumably succeeded inside the worker, their accumulated values (`results`) were discarded and are not observable from the outside. The only evidence they occurred is the `worker_invocations: 33` counter.\n\n**No retry.** Per instructions I did not re-run the 33-call probe. I confirmed fixture grounding with a single separate `read_file`: `alpha.txt` contains `\"Project Alpha: owner Ada; open bugs 3; deadline Friday.\"` (receipt `sha256:e833984d…`).\n\n**Feedback.** I filed a `bug` note observing that the limit error is opaque (conflating \"unavailable tool\" with \"limit exceeded\"), that the limit is enforced as a non-catchable abort rather than a Python exception, and that accumulated partial results are not surfaced despite documentation suggesting earlier results are retained.",
